welcome to the daily climate show coming  up today we'll be live in davos where  international leaders are grappling with  how to tackle climate change and the  global repercussions of the war in  ukraine  anthony albanese is sworn in as  australia's new prime minister with  action on climate change at top of the  agenda  and a senior safety consultant has  resigned from the energy giant shell  saying the company is causing extreme  harm to the planet  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to the world hear from the  people affected by those changes and  tried to get answers from those tasked  with tackling the problem  now alex sharma the president of the cop  26 climate summit has told sky news that  the crisis in ukraine will accelerate  the move away from dependence on fossil  fuels now climate is traditionally one  of the big issues that leaders try to  grapple with at the annual world  economic forum meeting in switzerland  but after russia's invasion of ukraine  the world's focus is elsewhere yet the  un has warned again and again this year  that we're not doing enough to avoid  catastrophic climate change well joining  me now from davos is our business  correspondent paul kelso and paul i know  you managed to grab a word with alok  sharma earlier he's determined isn't he  that climate change isn't sidelined at  this year's summit  yes he is he's actually probably the  ranking british politician in davos this  week there's no senior ministerial  presence no chance of the exchequer no  business secretary and as far as we know  no prime minister balak sharma is here  in his role as cop 26 president and  you're writing what you say in that  perhaps the headline attention has  shifted away from climate certainly in  the six months since glasgow and cop 26  yet about a third of the panels the  talking shops that go on here in this  rather rare refried conference  environment are about climate i suspect  that the agenda was put together before  the russian invasion of ukraine and the  consequences of that act of aggression  are that the immediate attention of  political leaders business leaders and  indeed civil society that's here is to  try and deal with uh the fallout from  what's happening just a couple of  thousand kilometers to the east but  speaking to alex sharma today he's here  trying to keep the renewable  flame to people's feet if you like to  try and keep up the pressure to get  people to deliver on the commitments  they made in glasgow and he told me that  he believes the war in ukraine will in  fact accelerate the shift to renewables  it's completely understandable that uh  as a result of russia's uh brutal and  illegal invasion of ukraine uh you are  seeing uh people focused uh on that  particular challenge uh that's  completely understandable of course we  have to stand shoulder to shoulder with  the people of ukraine but i think what  it is also done is highlighted the  vulnerability of countries relying on  fossil fuels particularly when they're  reliant on um hostile actors if i can  put it like that  and i think what you've seen in response  from countries around the world is a  realization that the way you ensure  energy security uh is to make sure that  you have homegrown clean energy you're  seeing that in the uk we've set out our  british energy security strategy very  clearly focused on renewables nuclear  clean energy you're seeing that coming  through from other countries as well  i've also spoken to the egyptian foreign  minister who's here because egypt  assumes the presidency of cop in  november and will host cop 27 in sharm  al-shaikh next year he  praised mr sharma's work he said they  were ready to take on the baton from the  uk and would continue the diplomatic  work it would be interesting to have an  african country hosting copy insisted it  wasn't an african cop but here you have  a a continent  that hasn't enjoyed the benefits of the  carbon age is feeling the impact of  climate change perhaps more immediately  than europe but clearly from what has  happened in ukraine despite all the  optimism from mr sharma and indeed the  egyptians real politic real events will  always potentially get in the way of  this long road and necessary road  driving business and governments towards  net zero  paul thanks very much indeed  let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and australia  swore in a new prime minister today  after what was dubbed the climate  election labour leader anthony albanese  campaigned on stronger climate ambitions  than the outgoing pm scott morrison  pledging to cut emissions by 43 percent  by 2030 and reaching net zero by 2050.  an unprecedented number of independent  candidates were elected and they'll be  pivotal in holding the new government to  account on climate policy  in his victory speech this weekend  albanese pledged to end the climate wars  we can have an even better future  if we seize the opportunities that are  right there in front of us  the opportunity to shape change rather  than be shaped by it  and we can shape change more effectively  if we seek to unite people on that  journey of change  together we can end the climate wars  [Applause]  back in the uk the government has  launched a multi-billion pound scheme to  help reduce carbon emissions from  heating buildings homeowners can get  grants of up to five thousand pounds to  replace gas boilers with new low-carbon  heat pumps it's been met with criticism  however some experts say the fund is  only enough to help around 90 000 homes  across the country  a thick blanket of orange dust has  caused disruption across iraq the  sandstorm has led to extremely low  visibility and air quality effectively  closing down the capital baghdad and  forcing many people to hospital with  breathing difficulties  iraq is prone to seasonal sandstorms but  experts have warned their growing  frequency has been brought on by  desertification record low rainfall  and climate change  now a safety consultant who spent more  than a decade working as a contractor  for shell has publicly cut ties claiming  the oil and gas company is causing  extreme harm to the planet in an email  to hundreds of shell employees including  the chief executive  caroline dennett wrote that the company  is planning to expand oil and gas  exploration against the advice of united  nations climate scientists and she ended  her email  it pains me to end this working  relationship which i have greatly valued  and i can no longer work for a company  that ignores all the alarms and  dismisses the risks of climate change  and ecological collapse  well shell sent this statement in  response they said be in no doubt we are  determined to deliver on our global  strategy to be a net zero company by  2050 and thousands of our people are  working hard to achieve this we have set  targets for the short medium and long  term and have every intention of hitting  them we're already investing billions of  dollars in low-carbon energy although  the world will still need oil and gas  for decades to come in sectors that  can't be easily decarbonized  well i spoke to caroline dennett earlier  and asked her why she's cutting ties  with shell now  yeah i think it's because i really truly  believe that they're just ignoring the  risks of climate change and ecological  collapse um you know the alarm bells  have been ringing the united nations the  international energy agency all saying  that we must immediately cease with any  new oil and gas extractions and rapidly  transition to renewables for you know  for a safe uh future and a habitable  world and i just don't see that shadow  doing that in any meaningful way and  that really contradicts their  their ambition  for their safety ambitions which is  around do no harm but they are causing  harms on a massive scale because of  their uh disregard for the climate risks  and you know the ecological problems  that the oil and gas industry's uh  producing  you say they're ignoring the alarms  around climate change but they would  point out wouldn't they that they've got  a net zero target they aim to hit net  zero by 2050 they say they've also got  short medium and long term targets all  of which they intend to hit and they're  throwing billions into it  yeah they do say that but i i i  find it hard to believe that  if you are having that target that you  would still look for new uh oil and gas  licenses to take even more out of the  ground than what we have you know and  the science is absolutely clear we have  to leave it in the ground you know we  really cannot afford to increase global  heating any more than than it is going  to be already and if we continue to  extract more that's just going to put  more co2 into the atmosphere so i just  think there's a real contradiction in  what they say publicly and what they're  doing in reality well yes one of your  complaints has been hasn't it that you  say that shell isn't winding down its  oil and gas but they say believe they  believe that their oil production has  peaked they say they think it's been  going down since 2019.  yep that that may be the case for oil i  don't have though you know their  internal statistics on that but there is  certainly  um a race for more extraction projects  and for more licenses and i just would  have thought if you're serious about  transitioning that you would really stop  that you would create a new vision for  your company and lead us into that  future you know shell have been pioneers  in in the energy industry and i just  think it's a real shame that they're not  using all of their  their human and financial and technical  capital to uh  to to put us on the right path to a safe  future you know it just seems a shame  that they're really sticking their heels  in on this well yes i mean they say they  have plans to transition away from  fossil fuels to towards clean energy but  in the meantime  there the world does still need some oil  and gas because there are some sectors  that can't be easily decarbonized i mean  they've got a point there haven't they  yeah and i don't think anyone is saying  we need to turn the tap off right at  this moment we would obviously see  massive societal collapse if we if we  did that but i do think you know we need  to see evidence of that rapid transition  and on a scale uh that that is going to  save us and i just think they are a  company that has scale they are capable  of doing that they just need to um get  on with it but that i don't think they  are you know i think they're they're  still talking as if we have decades and  decades uh before we need to act you  know their 2050 target means that they  have to act now not wait until it's 20  45 or 20 49.  caroline thanks very much indeed  thank you  well with me now is our climate  correspondent hannah thomas peter so  hannah how significant is a resignation  like this it's very unusual i think to  hear from somebody so closely and so  recently involved with a major oil and  gas company of course it's come at a  time when the oil and gas industry is  under in intense scrutiny and facing  severe criticism not just for their  ongoing role in climate change you know  the un has said that fossil fuel needs  to stay in the ground there can't be any  new extraction but also  for enjoying record profits driven by  the energy crisis and the war in ukraine  which has prompted calls for a windfall  tax among other things  but the broader context remains this  that  this government the uk's government  believes that  oil and gas in particular are going to  be necessary in the coming decades you  simply cannot turn the tax off and you  can't turn the taps off even quickly  and that gas is a necessary transition  fuel as we push towards a net zero  future and that critically gas can be  extracted and those net zero targets  remain  you know intact so there is a  disagreement between people like ms  dennett and campaigners who say that  oil and gas companies are going to be  responsible in large part for the world  missing its global heating targets  and those who are you know right at the  face of this including the british  government trying to make policy around  this who say that the two things can be  achieved that we can continue to extract  oil and gas to an extent and we can  still continue to eventually reduce our  carbon emissions to zero by 2050. hannah  thanks very much indeed  and that's it for today thanks very much  for watching

